The full story:

This particular Archeologist came closer to dying more times than any other
character I've ever played.  Didn't get any decent armor until late, partly
because I chose to stick with leather and other spell-friendly garments,
and partly because two rust monsters and a gray ooze trashed my helm of
brilliance, gauntlets of power, and cloak of displacement, respectively.  
AC was only 5 at Minetown.

That was the location of the first close call.  After converting the altar,
I successfully took care of the priest through judicious use of magic
missile, but fell to the combined assault of a Woodland-elf and a mumak,
even after a healing prayer.  Fortunately, the amulet about my neck turned
out to be lifesaving.  Even so, I would be dropped to single-digit hit
points a couple of times before successfully locking the mumak in a room.

The rest of the Mines were pretty routine, except for Grimtooth in a bones
pile.  The next waystations were the altars on dlvls 8 and 9.  Dlvl 9 was
the base of operations and storage location for all the junk too heavy to
carry.  A quick trek to Sokoban netted the "oR instead of the BoH I was
hoping for.  Multiple sacrifices yielded Demonbane and Ogresmasher.  

At this point, I nearly lost the character entirely 

AC was down to 1 at that point, thanks mostly to the addition of an elven
shield.  With that I was able to clear out the Quest levels, but retreated
to the main dungeon before tackling the Minion of Huhehotl.

Peril next struck my character in the form of an air elemental.  I was
quickly engulfed, but was able to zap self with a wand of teleport. 
Unfortunately, I 'ported only a few squares away.  This process was
repeated about three or four times, reducing HP to single digits once more. 
Finally, I was able to lock the E in one half of the level and dig to the
down stairs.

My luck began to change when I found a bag of holding just lying about.  I
also picked up a magic lamp.  I used the wish to get a blessed magic marker
with which to write ?oEA, giving me a decent AC (-7).  I ran into the
Ludios portal on dlvl 21, but turned upward again, finally destroying that
pesky air elemental blocking my way.  I resumed the quest after being
gifted with Grayswandir and advancing saber to skilled.  The Minion was a
challenge, disintegrating my boots and shield, also summoning a few nasties
(green slime, cockatrice, red dragon, etc.), and eventually knocking me to
35 HP before I destroyed him.  

After that, things got easier.  I found a =oLev to traverse Medusa's lair,
and achieved SDSM and a silver saber from the castle.  I used the castle
wand to get a few of magic markers, (?oEA, ?oEW, ?oGeno, and a lucky
+oIdentify) as well as a spellbook of magic mapping, which came in handy
for the mazes.

A player in the Valley had Mojo, and I used the Ludios loot to pay Moloch's
priest for protection.  After that, it was a quick run to Vlad, locate the
vibrating square, hit the Wizard, and grab the Amulet.  (I lost my cold
resistance to a gremlin in there somewhere, but that never became an
issue.)  The Wiz wasn't much of a threat; with my #twoweaponing I was able
to kill him in two hits every time.  A bone devil on the way up summoned
Yeenoghu, but that was about the last significant event.

The planes were ridiculously easy with the Orb of Detection to guide me, and
my =oLev for Air and Fire.  The Astral Plane was no real trouble, as I
found the correct altar right away, but with a couple of spare "oLS, I
stuck around, killing the Riders and taming some of the nastier nasties. 
(Side note: About the funniest thing I've seen yet was when one of my pets
made a poor choice:  "The purple worm swallows Death. --More--  The purple
worm vomits violently!  The purple worm drops dead!")

I used up my amulets of lifesaving from choking on a melon (I was Satiated? 
Oops.) and when Famine shriveled me to fainting and proceeded to lay the
smack down.  Time to make a stylish exit.  I had previously wished up a
saddle, so I mounted my pet silver dragon, and escorted my guardian angel
and purple worm to a glorious triumph.

Goodbye Lynx the Demigod...

